{"name":"Jaffa Orange","hex":"#d87839","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"jaffa","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A type of sweet orange, normally seedless.","An impotent or infertile male."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/jaffa"},{"term":"orange","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An evergreen tree of the genus Citrus such as Citrus sinensis which yields oranges (the fruit).","Any round citrus fruit with a yellow-red colour when ripe and a sour-sweet taste; the fruit of the orange tree.\n Specifically, a sweet orange or Citrus sinensis."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having the colour of the fruit of an orange tree; yellowred; reddish-yellow."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/orange"}],"entries":[{"title":"Jaffa orange","blocks":[{"text":"The Jaffa orange is an orange variety with few seeds and a tough skin that make it highly exportable. It was developed by Palestinian farmers in mid-19th century Ottoman Palestine, and takes its name from the city of Jaffa where it was first produced for export. Jaffa oranges are known for their sweet taste."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/Shukoranges.jpg/330px-Shukoranges.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jaffa_orange","wikidata":"Q115167"},{"title":"Jaffa","blocks":[{"text":"Jaffa, also called Japho, Joppa or Joppe in English, is an ancient Levantine port city which is part of Tel Aviv-Yafo, Israel, located in its southern part. The city sits atop a naturally elevated outcrop on the Mediterranean coastline."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a3/ISR-2013-Aerial-Jaffa-Port_of_Jaffa.jpg/330px-ISR-2013-Aerial-Jaffa-Port_of_Jaffa.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jaffa","wikidata":"Q180294"},{"title":"Orange (colour)","blocks":[{"text":"Orange is the colour between yellow and red on the spectrum of visible light. The human eyes perceive orange when observing light with a dominant wavelength between roughly 585 and 620 nanometres. In traditional colour theory, it is a secondary colour of pigments, produced by mixing yellow and red. In the RGB colour model, it is a tertiary colour. It is named after the fruit of the same name."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d8/Delicatearch.png/330px-Delicatearch.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orange_(colour)","wikidata":"Q39338"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c4/Pomological_Watercolor_POM00006321.jpg/500px-Pomological_Watercolor_POM00006321.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c4/Pomological_Watercolor_POM00006321.jpg/1280px-Pomological_Watercolor_POM00006321.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c4/Pomological_Watercolor_POM00006321.jpg/500px-Pomological_Watercolor_POM00006321.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Illustration of a Jaffa orange by J. Marion Shull","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APomological_Watercolor_POM00006321.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/a/a5/Jaffa_Orange_brand_from_Sarona.jpg/250px-Jaffa_Orange_brand_from_Sarona.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/a/a5/Jaffa_Orange_brand_from_Sarona.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/a/a5/Jaffa_Orange_brand_from_Sarona.jpg/250px-Jaffa_Orange_brand_from_Sarona.jpg?utm_source=en.w